        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"hamsun-victoria-5fa7bf1d366f41a3\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"The imagined Victoria deliberately avows that she loves the gentleman and clarifies him as the intended object of her love. Her slow, distinct delivery supports a controlled truth-conditional declaration, not a reflex-like expressive discharge. Nothing in the utterance undertakes a future commitment; indeed, it ends with “farewell.”\",\"ambiguity\":\"The event is direct speech within the fictional ending Johannes is composing, so it is simultaneously written representation and an imagined utterance; “imagined” best identifies its status.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"imagined\",\"description\":\"A direct love declaration spoken by Victoria within the imagined fictional scene that Johannes writes as the concluding chapter of his manuscript.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Within Johannes’s authored scenario, Victoria arrives after the gentleman has bitterly assumed that she rejected him and chose another. Her declaration corrects that misunderstanding: “it is You whom I love.” The measured articulation and immediate farewell frame it as a belated, controlled confession rather than an impulsive outburst or relational undertaking. Because this is Johannes’s fiction, it may also embody his wishful projection, but that framing does not alter the represented utterance’s dominant truth-conditional force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Jeg elsker Dem; misforstå mig ikke mere, det er Dem jeg elsker” (“I love You; misunderstand me no longer, it is You whom I love”).\",\"supports\":\"The explicit avowal and clarification of its object strongly support truth-conditional force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She “uttaler ordene så langsomt og vel” (“pronounces the words so slowly and distinctly”).\",\"supports\":\"The controlled, deliberate delivery weighs against construing the words as a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The declaration concludes with “farvel!” and she immediately covers her face and leaves.\",\"supports\":\"The farewell and departure provide no substantial evidence of a commitment or relational undertaking, despite the emotional intensity.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The scene is introduced as the final chapter Johannes already has in mind; afterward he puts down his pen and regards the book as finished.\",\"supports\":\"The love event is represented inside Johannes’s written imaginative composition rather than occurring directly in the passage’s primary narrative level.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as belonging to Knut Hamsun’s novel Victoria and as a valid explicit first-person love expression.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the work and the identification of the highlighted core expression.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately captures the core force: a deliberate avowal of love, without independent undertaking force or expressive/reflexive discharge. The metafictional framing is contextual rather than a missing core-force category.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
